  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "$\\int \\frac{10}{1-25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
  "worked_solution": "This integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}\u2061u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $25{x}^{2}={(5x)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{1-25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1-(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{artanh}\u2061u+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{artanh}\u20615x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|5x|<1.$",
